-- check/bad: the `.any` arm of §10.1's undetermined-receiver message (A.66,
-- §6.4). `decodeInto`'s constraint is on its RESULT, so nothing inside the
-- declaration determines `a` and only a use can. `always 1 (decodeInto 0)`
-- is a use that never does: the result is thrown away, and `ignored`'s own
-- annotation is `Int`, which does not mention `a`, so `promote` cannot
-- claim the constraint into the interface either. What is left is a slot
-- `settleUndetermined` must answer, and `fromInt` is not well known — `eq`
-- and `compare` mean the same thing at every type and the A.53 bridge
-- answers them, a user's own method does not and inventing one would be
-- inventing a meaning.
--
-- The other two `kind` arms are pinned elsewhere: `.number` by
-- `check/bad/WhereNonWellKnownAtLiteral`. Return-type dispatch is the
-- natural producer of `.any`, because a constraint that never reaches an
-- argument has nothing but the caller to pin it.
pub decodeInto : Int → a
    where a.fromInt : Int → a
decodeInto n = a.fromInt n


pub ignored : Int
ignored = always 1 (decodeInto 0)
